FAILPOINTS=enableTestAPI,repairFetchCreateTable (harness only; no fault injected into index data)

SOURCE_PROOF:
  pkg/ddl/executor.go:5971-6060 RepairTable builds a new TableInfo from user CREATE TABLE,
    preserves old table/index IDs, and has a TODO that the new TableInfo should be verified
    with actual data.
  pkg/ddl/executor.go:6023-6041 compares columns by name/type and indexes by name/columns/type.
    It does not compare index PrefixLen, Unique, Invisible, or other physical encoding options.
  pkg/ddl/index.go:3645-3668 getIndexInfoByNameAndColumn/indexColumnSliceEqual matches only
    index name and column names; index prefix length is ignored.

CONTROL_EXACT_REPAIR:
  Physical table t_exact was created with KEY idx_v(v(3)) and rows
    (1,'abc-one'),(2,'abc-two'),(3,'def-three').
  ADMIN REPAIR TABLE t_exact CREATE TABLE t_exact (... KEY idx_v(v(3))) succeeded.
  IGNORE INDEX and FORCE INDEX both returned id=2 for v='abc-two'.
  ADMIN CHECK TABLE t_exact was silent.

RED_PREFIX_MISMATCH:
  Physical table t was created with KEY idx_v(v(3)) and the same three rows.
  ADMIN REPAIR TABLE t CREATE TABLE t (... KEY idx_v(v(2))) succeeded and published:
    SHOW CREATE TABLE reports KEY idx_v(v(2)).
  IGNORE INDEX(idx_v) WHERE v='abc-two' returned id=2.
  FORCE INDEX(idx_v) WHERE v='abc-two' returned no rows.
  FORCE INDEX prefix LIKE 'abc%' still returned ids 1 and 2, so the failure is an equality
    lookup mismatch rather than a missing table row.
  COUNT(*) table scan and index scan were both 3; ADMIN CHECK TABLE t was silent.

RED_NONUNIQUE_TO_UNIQUE:
  Physical table t_nonunique was created with KEY idx_v(v) and rows
    (1,'abc-one'),(2,'abc-two'),(3,'abc-one').
  ADMIN REPAIR TABLE t_nonunique CREATE TABLE t_nonunique (... UNIQUE KEY idx_v(v))
    succeeded and published UNIQUE KEY idx_v(v).
  INSERT (4,'abc-one') succeeded even though the published metadata says UNIQUE.
  Table scan for v='abc-one' returned ids 1,3,4.
  FORCE INDEX(idx_v) returned only id=4.
  The default plan was Point_Get on idx_v and returned only id=4.
  ADMIN CHECK TABLE t_nonunique was silent.

RED_UNIQUE_TO_NONUNIQUE:
  Physical table t_unique was created with UNIQUE KEY uk_v(v), rows (1,'abc-one'),(2,'abc-two').
  ADMIN REPAIR TABLE t_unique CREATE TABLE t_unique (... KEY uk_v(v)) succeeded.
  Duplicate abc-one rows could then be inserted; table and index paths diverged from the
  original uniqueness contract while ADMIN CHECK TABLE remained silent.

INTERPRETATION:
  The system reuses an existing physical index ID/data while publishing a different logical
  index definition. A normal query can therefore select an index whose physical encoding or
  uniqueness semantics do not match TableInfo. This is a high-consequence candidate, but the
  product contract for operator-supplied ADMIN REPAIR definitions must be stated before filing
  upstream: the system should reject an unverified mismatch or clearly require an exact physical
  definition. It is distinct from ADD INDEX downscale and FLASHBACK FK identity drift.
